# G11-C closeout (review G11-C-IMPL-REVIEW-Alice §四.3): the test matrix the
# implementation review named as missing.  Every case here corresponds to a
# blocking item B1-B7, the explicit hint ①, or one of the §四.3 named coverage
# gaps (manifest matrix / direct LinkerResult.Placement check / merged-align
# execution / whole-group P-5 priority / non-canonical SECTION + sized NOTYPE
# / FIXED x G8 retry / FIXED x dynamic-split), and is asserted against the
# PRODUCT output -- the frozen wording, the LinkerResult.Placement contract
# via the MCS251_PLACEMENT_DUMP instrument, the produced ELF/map bytes.
#
# Fixture provenance: hand-built yaml2obj v2 objects (the edata-window /
# xseg-split pattern: the v1 flag word plus seteflags.py rewriting e_flags
# in place).  NOTE records follow the frozen v1 layout; the layout_hash
# values written into each record are the independently recomputed
# SHA-256-truncated H(F) of the record's own explicit fields (the negative
# cases never rely on the linker's own hash, which is not consulted).
#
# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: yaml2obj base.yaml -o base-v1.o
# RUN: %python seteflags.py base-v1.o && mv base-v1.o base.o
# RUN: yaml2obj bind-fx.yaml -o bfx-v1.o
# RUN: %python seteflags.py bfx-v1.o && mv bfx-v1.o bfx.o
# RUN: yaml2obj bind-align4.yaml -o bfa-v1.o
# RUN: %python seteflags.py bfa-v1.o && mv bfa-v1.o bfa.o
# RUN: yaml2obj other-same-stable.yaml -o oss-v1.o
# RUN: %python seteflags.py oss-v1.o && mv oss-v1.o oss.o
# RUN: yaml2obj owned-2nd-entity.yaml -o o2e-v1.o
# RUN: %python seteflags.py o2e-v1.o && mv o2e-v1.o o2e.o
# RUN: yaml2obj named-section-anchor.yaml -o nsa-v1.o
# RUN: %python seteflags.py nsa-v1.o && mv nsa-v1.o nsa.o
# RUN: yaml2obj entity-mismatch.yaml -o emm-v1.o
# RUN: %python seteflags.py emm-v1.o && mv emm-v1.o emm.o
# RUN: yaml2obj section-align-mismatch.yaml -o sam-v1.o
# RUN: %python seteflags.py sam-v1.o && mv sam-v1.o sam.o

# =====================================================================
# B1: NOTE reader boundary completeness -- pad truncation and non-zero
# padding.  The mutations are pure byte edits of a legal object, so the
# ONLY difference from the passing base.o link is the record tail.
# =====================================================================
# --- B1a: the last padding byte is non-zero (structurally well-formed
# --- ELF; the old reader accepted it because the total size still matched).
# RUN: %python note-mutate.py pad base.o b1-pad.o
# RUN: not mcs251-lld b1-pad.o --area-start=CSEG=0xff0200 --area-start=XSEG=0x010000 -o b1a.elf 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=B1PAD
# B1PAD: malformed placement NOTE in b1-pad.o: record at offset 20 has non-zero padding
# RUN: test ! -f b1a.elf
# --- B1b: the last padding byte is dropped and sh_size/descsz shrink with
# --- it.  The old reader advanced past the section end, underflowed the
# --- unsigned remaining-size and reported a schema byte read OUTSIDE the
# --- NOTE; the fixed reader must report the truncation itself.
# RUN: %python note-mutate.py cut base.o b1-trunc.o
# RUN: not mcs251-lld b1-trunc.o --area-start=CSEG=0xff0200 --area-start=XSEG=0x010000 -o b1b.elf 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=B1TRUNC
# B1TRUNC: malformed placement NOTE in b1-trunc.o: truncated record at offset 20 (record_size 28 does not fit the remaining NOTE data)
# B1TRUNC-NOT: unsupported schema version
# RUN: test ! -f b1b.elf
# --- B1c: the same object unmutated still links (positive control).
# RUN: mcs251-lld base.o --area-start=CSEG=0xff0200 --area-start=XSEG=0x010000 -o b1ok.elf && test -f b1ok.elf

# =====================================================================
